  "response": "The ego depletion hypothesis entered experimental psychology through a single vivid demonstration. Roy Baumeister and colleagues published an influential ego depletion study in 1998, and only after that publication did the field come to treat its central result as a benchmark: in the 1998 study, participants who resisted chocolate cookies and ate radishes gave up sooner on unsolvable puzzles. That behavioral pattern arose because the ego depletion model proposed that self-control draws on a limited resource that becomes depleted with use. On this account, the radish-eaters had spent their reserves resisting temptation and had less left for persistence. The framing was simple, intuitive, and readily extended to dieting, emotion regulation, and decision fatigue. Hundreds of laboratory experiments followed, most of them using a sequential-task design in which an initial act of restraint preceded a second measure of self-control.\n\nThe question of whether the effect was genuine therefore became a question about how to aggregate that literature. A 2010 meta-analysis by Martin Hagger and colleagues reported an average ego depletion effect size of about d = 0.62. By conventional standards this is a medium-to-large effect, and many readers took it as settled even though the 2010 Hagger meta-analysis included only twelve studies. The more consequential development came from examining how effect sizes related to sample sizes. Evan Carter and Michael McCullough reported evidence of small-study effects in the ego depletion literature in 2014. In that analysis, the smallest experiments tended to report the largest effects, a pattern that should not appear if all studies were estimating the same true effect. The small-study effects indicate that at least one of two distortions, perhaps both, shaped the record: publication bias inflated the effect sizes reported in the early ego depletion literature, or flexible analytic practices inflated the effect sizes reported in the early ego depletion literature. Selective publication filters out null results after the fact. Flexible analysis, by contrast, manufactures significant results before submission through choices about exclusions, covariates, and dependent measures.\n\nCorrection methods were designed to estimate what the literature would show in the absence of such filtering. Because selective publication had removed small null studies from view, bias-correction techniques such as PET-PEESE yielded ego depletion estimates near zero. These procedures regress observed effect sizes on their standard errors and extrapolate to a hypothetical study of infinite precision. Where the uncorrected average had suggested a robust phenomenon, the extrapolated value sat close to the origin. Those near-zero estimates confirm the conclusion that Carter and colleagues' 2015 meta-analysis determined that bias-corrected estimates provided no reliable evidence for the ego depletion effect. That determination settled the tension surrounding the 2010 figure. However large the average reported by Hagger and colleagues had been, a reanalysis sensitive to small-study effects showed that the figure reflected the composition of the literature rather than the strength of the underlying phenomenon.\n\nMeta-analytic correction, however, depends on modeling assumptions, so the field also turned to preregistered replication. In the 2016 Registered Replication Report, two dozen laboratories ran a common protocol with fixed analysis plans. Either the 2016 Registered Replication Report found a pooled ego depletion effect that was not statistically significant, or the 2016 Registered Replication Report found a statistically significant pooled ego depletion effect; the two outcomes cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. The 2016 Registered Replication Report was coordinated by Daniel Kahneman, and because of that coordination, the authors of the 2016 Registered Replication Report concluded that the pooled ego depletion effect was close to zero. Defenders of the resource model objected that the protocol's depletion manipulation was too weak. That objection motivated a further test using procedures chosen by proponents themselves.\n\nThat test arrived on a considerably larger scale. The 2021 Vohs multisite study included roughly 3,500 participants. In other words, about 3,500 people took part in the 2021 Vohs multisite ego depletion study, a sample large enough to detect effects far smaller than those claimed in the early laboratory work. Its estimate was small and statistically fragile. Taken together, the correction-based meta-analyses and the preregistered multisite designs led most researchers to judge that the original effect sizes owed more to how studies were reported than to any depletable reserve of self-control.",
